{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"To keep the same I‑IV‑V feel but sing lower, move the progression from E A B down a whole step to D G A. The three chords you’re playing (E A B) line up perfectly with the key of E major, where they function as the I (E‑G#‑B), IV (A‑C#‑E) and V (B‑D#‑F#) chords – three of the four diatonic chords listed for the E‑major key in the data. Because you want to lower the vocal range, transpose every chord down a whole tone: E becomes D (D‑F#‑A), A becomes G (G‑B‑D), and B becomes A (A‑C#‑E). In the new key of D major the chords are I (D‑F#‑A), IV (G‑B‑D) and V (A‑C#‑E), preserving the same functional relationship. \r\n\r\n**Why E major fits best**: The top‑match list shows E major with 3/4 of the supplied chords diatonic, meaning all three of your chords belong naturally to that key. A‑major, C#‑minor and F#‑minor also show 3/4 matches, but they would require a non‑diatonic B‑major chord (in A‑major B is V, but the presence of E as the I‑chord points directly to E‑major). \r\n\r\n**How to tell the key by ear**: Listen for the chord that feels like “home” or resolves most satisfyingly; in E‑major that’s the E chord (the I). When you hear the progression settle on D after transposition, that D chord becomes the new home base, confirming the key of D‑major. \r\n\r\n**Roman‑numeral analysis in the original key (E‑major)**: \r\n- E major = I (E‑G#‑B) \r\n- A major = IV (A‑C#‑E) \r\n- B major = V (B‑D#‑F#) \r\n\r\n**Roman‑numeral analysis after transposition (D‑major)**: \r\n- D major = I (D‑F#‑A) \r\n- G major = IV (G‑B‑D) \r\n- A major = V (A‑C#‑E) \r\n\r\n**Scales**: \r\n- E major scale: E F# G# A B C# D# \r\n- D major scale (your new key): D E F# G A B C# \r\n\r\nPlay the transposed chords and let your vocal range sit more comfortably while the harmonic function stays exactly the same.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":4918,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":4,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":4,"id":"f-1","kind":null,"lab