{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes, the chords **E** (E‑G♯‑B) and **C** (C‑E‑G) share the note **E**.  \r\n\r\nBoth chords contain three notes each: **E major** is built from the root E, the major third G♯, and the perfect fifth B, while **C major** consists of the root C, the major third E, and the perfect fifth G. The common pitch class in these two triads is the note E, which appears as the root of the E chord and as the third of the C chord. (If you consider the **E minor** triad—E‑G‑B—it also overlaps with C major on the notes E and G.)","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":1633,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":3,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":4,"id":"f-12","kind":null,"label":"skill.commontones","match":null,"prompt":"do E and C have any notes in common at all","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":2}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The distance from G up to E is called a **major sixth**.  \r\n\r\nA major sixth spans six letter names (G‑A‑B‑C‑D‑E) and contains nine semitones, which is the interval size for a major sixth. In diatonic harmony this interval often appears in chords that add the sixth degree to a triad.  \r\n\r\n**Example chords that contain a G‑to‑E major sixth:**\r\n\r\n- **G6** (G‑B‑D‑E) – a G major triad with the added major sixth (E).  \r\n- **Cmaj9** (C‑E‑G‑B‑D) – the E in the chord is a major sixth above the G (the fourth of the chord).  \r\n- **Em7♭5** (E‑G‑B♭‑D) – the E is the root, and the G‑to‑E interval inside the chord is a minor third, but when the chord is voiced over a G bass it yields a major sixth above that bass.  \r\n\r\nSo whenever you see G and E together with G as the lower note, you’re hearing a major sixth interval.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":2504,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":0,"id":"f-20","kind":null,"label":"skill.interval","match":null,"prompt":"from G up to E what do you call that distance","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["G6 (G-B-D-E)"],"verdict":"F","verified":1}
{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"In D major the diatonic chords are **D (D F♯ A), Em (E G B), F♯m (F♯ A C♯), G (G B D), A (A C♯ E), Bm (B D F♯), and C♯dim (C♯ E G).**  \r\n\r\nThese seven chords cover every degree of the D‑major scale and form the basis for most progressions in the key.  \r\n\r\n**How they’re used**  \r\n- **I–IV–V** (D – G – A) is the classic tonic‑subdominant‑dominant loop.  \r\n- **ii–V–I** (Em – A – D) gives a jazzy resolution; adding the 7ths (Em7 – A7 – Dmaj7) smooths voice leading.  \r\n- **vi–IV–I–V** (Bm – G – D – A) is a common pop sequence.  \r\n- The **vii° (C♯dim)** works as a leading‑tone chord that resolves naturally to the tonic D.  \r\n\r\n**Common extensions and useful variations**  \r\n\r\n| Diatonic chord | Common extension | Notes (including 7ths) |\r\n|----------------|------------------|------------------------|\r\n| D maj7 | Dmaj7 | D F♯ A C♯ |\r\n| D6 | D6 | D F♯ A B |\r\n| Em7 | Em7 | E G B D |\r\n| F♯m7 | F♯m7 | F♯ A C♯ E |\r\n| G6 | G6 | G B D E |\r\n| A7 | A7 | A C♯ E G |\r\n| Bm7 | Bm7 | B D F♯ A |\r\n| C♯dim7 | C♯dim7 | C♯ E G B♭ |\r\n\r\n**Secondary dominants and borrowed chords**  \r\n\r\n- **E7 (Vⁱ⁄ᴵ)** → resolves to **A** (the V of D).  \r\n- **B7 (Vⁱ⁄ᴵᵥ)** → resolves to **Em** (the ii chord).  \r\n- **G♭maj7** (♭II) can be used for a colourful “tritone‑substitution” feel before the A7.\r\n\r\n**Typical open‑shape voicings**  \r\n\r\n- **D major (open)**: x‑x‑0‑2‑3‑2  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (2/4.2/3.0/2.3/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n- **G major (open)**: 3‑2‑0‑0‑0‑3  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\n- **A7 (open)**: x‑0‑2‑0‑2‑0  \r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/5.2/4.0/3.2/1)\r\n```  \r\n\r\nUse these shapes as a starting point, then experiment with the extensions above to add richness and tension while staying firmly in the key of D major.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3398,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":17,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":3,"id":"f-28","kind":null,"label":"skill.diatonicchords","match":null,"prompt":"chords that work in D major?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":["Dmaj7 | D F# A C#","D6 | D F# A B","G6 | G B D E","A7 | A C# E G","C#dim7 | C# E G Bb"],"verdict":"F","verified":7}
