{"agent":"skill.keyidentification","answer":"Transposed up a whole step, the progression becomes Bm (B D F#), G (G B D), D (D F# A), A (A C# E). The original set Am – F – C – G fits perfectly in both C major and its relative minor, A minor, because each chord appears in the diatonic list for those keys (see the data: “C major … Diatonic set: C, Dm, Em, F, G, Am, Bdim” and “A minor … Diatonic set: Am, Bdim, C, Dm, Em, F, G”). Since the two keys are tied, you can tell which one you’re really in by listening for the tonic (home) chord – if the music feels resolved on C, you’re in C major; if it feels resolved on Am, you’re in A minor. In C major the Roman‑numeral functions are: Am = vi, F = IV, C = I, G = V. In A minor they are: Am = i, F = VI, C = III, G = VII. The natural scales are C major: C D E F G A B and A minor: A B C D E F G. So whether you think of the progression as vi‑IV‑I‑V in C major or i‑VI‑III‑VII in A minor, the same chord shapes apply, and moving everything up a whole step gives you the Bm‑G‑D‑A version shown above.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":3204,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":5,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":10,"id":"f-73","kind":null,"label":"skill.transpose","match":null,"prompt":"put Am F C G up a whole step pls","routing":"orchestrator-skill-semantic","row":"question","said":null,"set":"fresh-test","source_author":null,"source_license":null,"source_link":null,"unchecked":[],"verdict":"T","verified":5}
