{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"The chord D–F–A♯ is a **D minor‑augmented‑fifth (D m(♯5))**, which typically functions as a leading‑tone (vii°) chord that resolves to the tonic in minor keys.\r\n\r\n**Explanation**\r\n\r\n- **Notes:** D (root), F (minor third), A♯ (augmented fifth).  \r\n- **Name:** Because it contains a minor third (D–F) and a raised fifth (A♯), the proper label is D m(♯5) – sometimes written D♭⁷♭9♭13 in jazz contexts, but the simplest spelling is D m(♯5).  \r\n- **Harmonic function:** In a minor key, the seventh scale degree (the leading tone) forms a diminished triad that wants to resolve up a half step to the tonic. By raising the fifth, the chord gains extra tension, heightening its pull to the tonic. Thus, in a key such as **C minor (C–E♭–G)**, D m(♯5) would act as **vii°/i**, leading back to **C minor (C–E♭–G)**.  \r\n- **Common uses:** This chord often appears as a chromatic passing chord, a substitution for a dominant, or part of a secondary‑dominant progression (e.g., ii⁷/V → V → I). Its distinctive sound comes from the clash between the minor third and the raised fifth, creating a strong sense of resolution when moving to the tonic or a related chord.","chatbot_error":null,"chatbot_ms":9026,"chatbot_status":200,"claim":null,"claims":2,"ga":null,"ga_errors":0,"ga_ms":1,"id":"m-37","kind":null,"label":"ambiguous","match":null,"prompt":"What is the name and function of the D-F-A♯ chord in this guitar score?","routing":"low-confidence-fallback","row":"question","said":null,"set":"music-se","source_author":"Tikhon","source_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source_link":"https://music.stackexchange.com/questions/144196/what-is-the-name-and-function-of-the-d-f-a%e2%99%af-chord-in-this-guitar-score","unchecked":[],"verdict":"F","verified":1}
